---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "manage",
 "name": "Namecheap",
 "title": "How to Add and Manage DNS Records in Namecheap",
 "description": "Add, edit, and delete DNS records in Namecheap step by step, with the gotchas that break sites and mail, and free tools to verify every change.",
 "intro": "Managing DNS in Namecheap is a short walk once you know where the records live and which defaults to watch. This is the complete flow, with the verification step most guides skip.",
 "panel": "Namecheap account, Domain List, Manage next to the domain, then the Advanced DNS tab. Host Records is the table where A, CNAME, MX, TXT and friends live.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Open Advanced DNS",
   "Domain List, Manage, Advanced DNS tab."
  ],
  [
   "Add the host record",
   "Add New Record: choose type, set Host (@ for root, www for the alias, or a subdomain), Value, and TTL (60 minimum, 5min default is fine)."
  ],
  [
   "Mind the toggle",
   "At the top, Nameservers must be Namecheap BasicDNS / PremiumDNS / Custom DNS. If it says Web Hosting DNS, records on this tab are not what the domain uses."
  ],
  [
   "Verify",
   "Confirm live values with the record lookup before declaring victory."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "The Web Hosting DNS vs BasicDNS/Custom DNS dropdown silently changes which record set is live. Records edited under the wrong mode do nothing.",
  "fieldMap": { "name": "Host", "value": "Value", "ttl": "5 min to 24 h presets", "apex": "@", "note": "Namecheap offers TTL presets; the shortest is 5 minutes. Advanced DNS is where records live." },
"faqs": [
  [
   "Minimum TTL on Namecheap?",
   "60 seconds, handy for migrations. Raise it back to 5 minutes or more afterwards."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Namecheap?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Namecheap's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for {name} to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Namecheap?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Namecheap?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in {name}, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
